  "id": "COMPOUND:17374 - PROTEIN:6336",
  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N is implicated in the regulation of Thrombopoietin through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Mollicellin N acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid and Ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal hydrolase 5, which in turn target Thrombopoietin. This indicates a potential pathway where Mollicellin N may modulate the activity of these intermediates, ultimately influencing Thrombopoietin levels. The presence of multiple targets and their shared interaction with 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid suggests a possible regulatory mechanism involving the enzyme's role in the biosynthesis or degradation of this compound. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the molecular interactions, with Mollicellin N potentially serving as a key regulator in the Thrombopoietin signaling cascade. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Mollicellin N and Thrombopoietin, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
